{{- if model.manifest.database != null -}}
# Database (PostgreSQL)

The platform already connects to PostgreSQL for you. You do **not** write connection, password or health-check code. You fill in settings, then add your tables and migrations.

Platform code lives in `Infrastructure/Database/` and `Data/{{ model.names.dot_net_class_name }}DbContext.cs`. Do not edit it. If something is missing, raise a platform ticket.

## What the platform provides

- One PostgreSQL connection setup (`AddForgeDatabase`, already called in `Program.cs`).
- The user name and password read from AWS Secrets Manager, refreshed automatically after a password rotation.
- A readiness check at `/health/ready` that runs `SELECT 1`.
{{- if model.manifest.tenant_ready }}
- `{{ model.names.dot_net_class_name }}DbContext`, built on Core `TenantDbContext`: every tenant-owned query is limited to the caller's tenant and every write is checked. See [`Tenancy.md`](Tenancy.md).
- The `tenants` table and its first row, created by the `AddTenants` migration. Run `dotnet ef database update` before the first request.
{{- else }}
- `{{ model.names.dot_net_class_name }}DbContext`, with tenant filtering for entities that implement `ITenantEntity`.
{{- end }}
- The tooling for EF Core migrations (`dotnet ef` works out of the box).

PostgreSQL is the only supported engine for now.

## Settings

| Setting | What to put there |
|---|---|
| `ConnectionStrings:Default` | Host, database, port and SSL mode, for example `Host=<endpoint>;Port=5432;Database=<db>;SSL Mode=VerifyFull`. For local development `appsettings.Development.json` already has a localhost connection string with a user name and password. |
| `Forge:Database:SecretId` | The Secrets Manager secret (name or ARN) that holds the database credentials. Leave empty for local development. |
| `Forge:Database:RootCertificate` | Path to the CA file used to verify the server. Use it with `SSL Mode=VerifyFull`. |

With `SecretId` set, the user name and password come from the secret and anything you put in the connection string for them is replaced. The secret must look like this (RDS-managed secrets named `rds!db-...` and standard rotation secrets already do):

```json
{ "username": "app_user", "password": "..." }
```

Other keys in the secret are ignored.

Use `appsettings.<Environment>.json` or environment variables for per-environment values, for example `ConnectionStrings__Default` and `Forge__Database__SecretId`.

### AWS permissions

The service's role needs `secretsmanager:GetSecretValue` on the secret, and `kms:Decrypt` if the secret uses a customer-managed key. A secret in another AWS account also needs a resource policy that allows the role.

### Certificates

The RDS CA bundle is at `https://truststore.pki.rds.amazonaws.com/global/global-bundle.pem`. Add the file to your container image and point `Forge:Database:RootCertificate` at it. The generated `Dockerfile` does not include it yet.

## Password rotation

When PostgreSQL rejects the password, the platform reads the secret again and retries opening the connection **once**. Requests that fail at the same moment share one refresh, and refreshes happen at most every 5 seconds. Only opening the connection is retried. No command or transaction is ever replayed.

Limits:
- Only the **password** can change while the service runs. If a rotation also changes the user name, restart the service.
- Changes to host, database or other connection settings need a restart.

## Health endpoints

| Endpoint | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `/health` | The service is alive. It never touches the database, so a short database outage does not make containers restart. The Dockerfile health check uses it. |
| `/health/ready` | The service can reach the database. Use it for load balancer readiness. |

## Adding tables

Add an entity and a configuration class in your feature folder. The platform finds every `IEntityTypeConfiguration<T>` in the project, so you never edit the platform DbContext to add a table.

```csharp
// Domains/Billing/Data/InvoiceEntity.cs
namespace {{ model.names.namespace }}.Domains.Billing.Data;

public sealed class InvoiceEntity
{
    public required Guid Id { get; set; }
    public required string Number { get; set; }
    public required DateTimeOffset CreatedAt { get; set; }
}
```

```csharp
// Domains/Billing/Data/InvoiceConfiguration.cs
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Metadata.Builders;

namespace {{ model.names.namespace }}.Domains.Billing.Data;

public sealed class InvoiceConfiguration : IEntityTypeConfiguration<InvoiceEntity>
{
    public void Configure(EntityTypeBuilder<InvoiceEntity> builder)
    {
        builder.ToTable("invoices");
        builder.HasKey(e => e.Id);
        builder.Property(e => e.Number).HasMaxLength(50).IsRequired();
        builder.HasIndex(e => e.Number).IsUnique();
    }
}
```

The DbContext is a `partial` class, so you can add a `DbSet` from your own file:

```csharp
// Domains/Billing/Data/{{ model.names.dot_net_class_name }}DbContext.Billing.cs
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

namespace {{ model.names.namespace }}.Data;

public partial class {{ model.names.dot_net_class_name }}DbContext
{
    public DbSet<InvoiceEntity> Invoices => Set<InvoiceEntity>();
}
```

Services take the DbContext through the constructor and keep queries inside the service layer:

```csharp
public sealed class InvoiceService({{ model.names.dot_net_class_name }}DbContext dbContext)
{
    public Task<InvoiceEntity?> FindAsync(Guid id, CancellationToken cancellationToken) =>
        dbContext.Invoices.AsNoTracking().FirstOrDefaultAsync(i => i.Id == id, cancellationToken);
}
```

## Migrations (EF Core)

Run these from the solution folder. Install the tool once with `dotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef`.

```powershell
# 1. After changing entities, create a migration
dotnet ef migrations add AddInvoices --project src/{{ model.names.namespace }} --output-dir Data/Migrations

# 2. Produce the SQL the pipeline applies (safe to run more than once)
dotnet ef migrations script --idempotent --project src/{{ model.names.namespace }} --output migrations.sql

# 3. Local database only
dotnet ef database update --project src/{{ model.names.namespace }}
```

Rules:
- Commit the generated migration files. Review them in the pull request.
- Never edit a migration that has been applied anywhere. Add a new one.
- Apply migrations to test and production from the **deployment pipeline** (the idempotent script, or `dotnet ef migrations bundle`). Do not run them when the service starts.
- To undo a change, add a new migration that reverses it.
- Generating a migration or a script does not connect to a database. `database update` connects to the database in `ConnectionStrings__Default`.

## Tests

`tests/{{ model.names.test_project_name }}/DatabaseTests.cs` covers the platform rules (password rotation, secret credentials, registration and the readiness check) without needing a database. Keep it. Add your own tests for your queries in separate files.
{{- end -}}
